<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=microscope">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Microscope</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1620</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A microscope is an instrument for examining objects too small to be seen by the naked eye; the earliest known compound microscopes, which combine an objective lens near the specimen with an eyepiece, appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> around 1620. The inventor is unknown, and claims have been made for spectacle makers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and for Cornelis Drebbel. It remained largely a novelty until the 1660s and 1670s, when naturalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the Netherlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> began using it to study biology; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonie-van-leeuwenhoek">Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</a> reported the discovery of micro-organisms in 1676.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/microscope.jpg" alt="Microscope" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Les Chatfield from Brighton, England · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fine_rotative_table_Microscope_5_(12996283235).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Objects resembling lenses date back 4,000 years, and the earliest known use of simple microscopes, or magnifying glasses, dates to the widespread use of lenses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eyeglasses">eyeglasses</a> in the thirteenth century. Several claims for the compound microscope revolve around the spectacle-making centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, naming Hans Lippershey, who applied for the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a> patent in 1608, and the expatriate Cornelis Drebbel, who was noted to have a version in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1619.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a> seems to have found after 1610 that he could focus his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a> closely to view small objects. After seeing a compound microscope built by Drebbel exhibited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1624, he built his own improved version. Giovanni Faber coined the name microscopio in 1625 for the instrument Galileo submitted to the Accademia dei Lincei.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The first detailed account of the microscopic anatomy of organic tissue, Giambattista Odierna's The Fly's Eye, appeared in 1644. Marcello Malpighi began his analysis of biological structures with the lungs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-hooke">Robert Hooke</a>'s Micrographia, published in 1665, had a large impact, largely because of its illustrations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonie-van-leeuwenhoek">Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</a> achieved up to 300 times magnification with a simple single-lens instrument, rediscovered red blood cells and spermatozoa, and helped popularise the use of microscopes for biological microstructure.</p><p>In 1893 August Köhler developed Köhler illumination, which is central to reaching the theoretical limits of resolution. Phase contrast, discovered by Frits Zernike in 1953, and differential interference contrast, by Georges Nomarski in 1955, allowed unstained, transparent samples to be imaged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1931 Ernst Ruska and Max Knoll developed the first prototype electron microscope, which uses electrons in place of light, giving much higher resolution. Knoll developed the scanning electron microscope in 1935, and the first commercial one, the Stereoscan, was marketed in 1965. From 1981 to 1983 Gerd Binnig and Heinrich Rohrer at IBM in Zürich created the scanning probe microscope, and in 1986 the atomic force microscope was invented. Stefan Hell shared the 2014 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Chemistry for the STED technique.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Microscope” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hooke’s Micrographia (1665) and the letters of Antonie van Leeuwenhoek from Delft opened the world of the <b>microscope</b>; Christiaan Huygens designed the first pendulum clock and explained the ring of Saturn; Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz developed the calculus independently of Newton and published it first, in 1684.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2007 researchers at Stanford University reported that the Chinese purple pigment drew on knowledge gained by Taoist alchemists trying to make jade, and <b>microscope</b> marks give evidence of the earliest industrial use of lathes for polishing.</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1620</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Europe</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The inventor is unknown; claims have been made for Zacharias Janssen in the Netherlands, for Cornelis Drebbel and for Galileo.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/eyeglasses">Eyeglasses<span>c. 1290</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/germ-theory-of-disease">Germ theory of disease<span>1658</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cell-theory">Cell theory<span>1839</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=microscope">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
